    "id": "10.1002/lno.70554",
    "title": "Autotroph–heterotroph relationships shape cyanobacterial bloom dynamics revealed by metatranscriptomics",
    "abstract": "Abstract\nAccelerated eutrophication and climate change are driving freshwater cyanobacterial blooms worldwide. Cyanobacteria–bacteria interactions shape phycosphere nutrient cycling and bloom dynamics, but their temporal shifts during bloom progression remain poorly understood. Using metatranscriptomics, we investigated the interactions between Microcystis and phycospheric bacteria throughout a bloom season in Lake Taihu, a heavily eutrophic lake in eastern China. During the early bloom, Microcystis and phycospheric bacteria co‐expressed genes associated with phosphate metabolism and oxidative stress response, whereas genes involved in nitrogen and iron acquisition were co‐expressed during the late bloom, suggesting potential nutrient competition. Carbohydrate metabolism‐related interactions persisted across bloom stages. As the bloom progressed, the dominant interacting taxa shifted from Hyphomicrobiales to Burkholderiales. We further assessed the generality of these patterns using metatranscriptomic datasets spanning multiple years and lakes. Phosphorus‐related interactions were consistently enriched during early blooms, whereas nitrogen competition‐related patterns predominated during late blooms; oxidative stress response‐related interactions, however, were more variable. Together, these findings reveal stage‐dependent shifts in phycospheric interactions that may contribute to bloom development and decline. The recurrence of these patterns across spatial and temporal scales suggests that shifts in phycospheric interactions are a general feature of bloom progression and may help predict bloom responses to environmental change.\nAbstract\nAccelerated eutrophication and climate change are driving freshwater cyanobacterial blooms worldwide. Cyanobacteria–bacteria interactions shape phycosphere nutrient cycling and bloom dynamics, but their temporal shifts during bloom progression remain poorly understood. Using metatranscriptomics, we investigated the interactions between Microcystis and phycospheric bacteria throughout a bloom season in Lake Taihu, a heavily eutrophic lake in eastern China. During the early bloom, Microcystis and phycospheric bacteria co-expressed genes associated with phosphate metabolism and oxidative stress response, whereas genes involved in nitrogen and iron acquisition were co-expressed during the late bloom, suggesting potential nutrient competition. Carbohydrate metabolism-related interactions persisted across bloom stages. As the bloom progressed, the dominant interacting taxa shifted from Hyphomicrobiales to Burkholderiales. We further assessed the generality of these patterns using metatranscriptomic datasets spanning multiple years and lakes. Phosphorus-related interactions were consistently enriched during early blooms, whereas nitrogen competition-related patterns predominated during late blooms; oxidative stress response-related interactions, however, were more variable. Together, these findings reveal stage-dependent shifts in phycospheric interactions that may contribute to bloom development and decline. The recurrence of these patterns across spatial and temporal scales suggests that shifts in phycospheric interactions are a general feature of bloom progression and may help predict bloom responses to environmental change.",
