    "id": "10.1111/1462-2920.70479",
    "title": "Divergent Responses of Bacterial and Microeukaryotic Diversity to Water‐Level Fluctuations in a Lake‐Floodplain System",
    "abstract": "Water‐level fluctuations deliver soil‐derived taxa into the water column, yet bacteria and microeukaryotes respond divergently to flooding. This asymmetry leads to inconsistent diversity trends, with microeukaryotic richness increasing with water levels while bacterial richness remains unchanged. This pattern challenges the classic diversity pattern associated with water‐level fluctuations.\n\nABSTRACT\nWater‐level fluctuations (WLF) are widely accepted as key drivers of aquatic species diversity in lake‐floodplain systems, with diversity increasing as water levels rise. However, microorganisms may challenge this pattern that their richness does not necessarily increase with water levels. To resolve this inconsistency, we applied 16S and 18S rRNA gene amplicon sequencing to bacterial and microeukaryotic assemblages in Dongting Lake, China, during the low‐water (LWP; January 2024) and high‐water periods (HWP; August 2024). Microeukaryotic richness was higher during HWP than LWP (p  0.3), consistent with their divergent fates in the water column: soil microeukaryotes are more likely to persist after flooding than soil bacteria. Co‐occurrence networks evidenced substantially stronger bacteria‐microeukaryote associations at high water and partial least squares path modelling resolved a DOC‐to‐bacteria‐to‐microeukaryotes pathway activated only during the HWP. These signals suggest that enhanced hydrological connectivity promoted community coalescence, possibly through intensified trophic coupling, although this interpretation requires experimental verification. This study highlights that natural WLFs reshape microbial richness by intermittently reconnecting terrestrial and aquatic microbial species pools.\n\nWater-level fluctuations deliver soil-derived taxa into the water column, yet bacteria and microeukaryotes respond divergently to flooding. This asymmetry leads to inconsistent diversity trends, with microeukaryotic richness increasing with water levels while bacterial richness remains unchanged. This pattern challenges the classic diversity pattern associated with water-level fluctuations.\n\nABSTRACT\nWater-level fluctuations (WLF) are widely accepted as key drivers of aquatic species diversity in lake-floodplain systems, with diversity increasing as water levels rise. However, microorganisms may challenge this pattern that their richness does not necessarily increase with water levels. To resolve this inconsistency, we applied 16S and 18S rRNA gene amplicon sequencing to bacterial and microeukaryotic assemblages in Dongting Lake, China, during the low-water (LWP; January 2024) and high-water periods (HWP; August 2024). Microeukaryotic richness was higher during HWP than LWP (p  0.3), consistent with their divergent fates in the water column: soil microeukaryotes are more likely to persist after flooding than soil bacteria. Co-occurrence networks evidenced substantially stronger bacteria-microeukaryote associations at high water and partial least squares path modelling resolved a DOC-to-bacteria-to-microeukaryotes pathway activated only during the HWP. These signals suggest that enhanced hydrological connectivity promoted community coalescence, possibly through intensified trophic coupling, although this interpretation requires experimental verification. This study highlights that natural WLFs reshape microbial richness by intermittently reconnecting terrestrial and aquatic microbial species pools.",
