    "id": "10.1111/mec.70615",
    "title": "Symbiont‐Derived Key Innovations Across the Diversity and Diets of Herbivorous Insects",
    "abstract": "ABSTRACT\nWith prodigious biomass and diversity, herbivorous insects are among the world's most successful animals. It is widely believed, however, that some plant‐based diets do not meet the needs of insect metabolism. Microbial symbionts may balance such deficits through diverse biochemistry. Here, we consider whether obligate nutritional symbioses between insects and microbes support large portions of insect herbivore diversity. In doing so, we assess the evidence that these highly integrated relationships are key innovations, circumventing nutritive shortfalls within particular plant tissues. Toward these ends, we compile nutrient data for 20 plant‐ and non‐plant foods, plus data on diet and symbiosis across 874 families of insects—both herbivorous and non‐herbivorous. Our results reinforce widely believed, but often uncited evidence that several insect taxa have long‐consumed plant tissues with low concentrations of B‐vitamins, nitrogen, or accessible carbon. Emerging as symbiont‐enriched are seed‐feeders, which face insufficient quantities of several B‐vitamins in their food. Experimental and genome assessments show how symbionts surmount these B‐vitamin shortcomings, showing also where this same challenge, and those of high fibre or low amino acid content are surmounted through symbiosis on other diets. We thus show where symbiont services are key innovations. Over half of the world's insect herbivores persist without nutritive assistance from obligate symbionts. This is largely because many consume leaves, which are reasonably good sources of amino acids and B‐vitamins. Lateral transfer of microbial genes encoding plant cell‐wall degrading enzymes has helped to overcome high fibre content, defusing the most challenging nutritive aspect of leaf‐chewing diets.\nABSTRACT\nWith prodigious biomass and diversity, herbivorous insects are among the world's most successful animals. It is widely believed, however, that some plant-based diets do not meet the needs of insect metabolism. Microbial symbionts may balance such deficits through diverse biochemistry. Here, we consider whether obligate nutritional symbioses between insects and microbes support large portions of insect herbivore diversity. In doing so, we assess the evidence that these highly integrated relationships are key innovations, circumventing nutritive shortfalls within particular plant tissues. Toward these ends, we compile nutrient data for 20 plant- and non-plant foods, plus data on diet and symbiosis across 874 families of insects—both herbivorous and non-herbivorous. Our results reinforce widely believed, but often uncited evidence that several insect taxa have long-consumed plant tissues with low concentrations of B-vitamins, nitrogen, or accessible carbon. Emerging as symbiont-enriched are seed-feeders, which face insufficient quantities of several B-vitamins in their food. Experimental and genome assessments show how symbionts surmount these B-vitamin shortcomings, showing also where this same challenge, and those of high fibre or low amino acid content are surmounted through symbiosis on other diets. We thus show where symbiont services are key innovations. Over half of the world's insect herbivores persist without nutritive assistance from obligate symbionts. This is largely because many consume leaves, which are reasonably good sources of amino acids and B-vitamins. Lateral transfer of microbial genes encoding plant cell-wall degrading enzymes has helped to overcome high fibre content, defusing the most challenging nutritive aspect of leaf-chewing diets.",
